We have revised our draft and provided the following improvements:

(1) a more detailed discussion of the CI/CD background;

(2) additional defense evaluations;

(3) a comparison with a concurrent work (ArXiVed on October 5, 2025);

(4) an evaluation using more frequent magic strings, such as “www.google.com”;
 which appears in more than 7.1M GitHub projects; and

(5) insights for future defense directions.

We also want to clarify that **manually validating real-world patches is extremely challenging due to their complexity**. To illustrate this, we report the average and maximum numbers of patch lines and tokens in the following tables. In practice, reviewing a patch with up to **19,702 modified lines** makes it nearly impossible for developers to reliably assess its correctness or security. **This is why platforms like GitHub rely on CI/CD pipelines to automatically verify patch correctness**. However, SWExploit can bypass these CI/CD checks, revealing the limitations of current CI/CD-based security validation mechanisms when evaluating agent-generated patches.


| Complexity Metric | Avg. | Max    |
| ----------------- | ---- | ------ |
| Patch Line        | 764  | 19702  |
| Patch Tokens      | 7732 | 224677 |
